     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 2, \"rationale\": \"Deploying mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin together blocks exfiltration, and no single control does. Why exfiltration is otherwise reachable: phishing is always available and gives foothold_workstation plus stolen_user. Without segmentation, pivot_internal then gives reach_internal. auth_appserver works because a workstation foothold bypasses mfa_user. kerberoast then yields stolen_admin. Why the pair works: mfa:admin blocks auth_dc and auth_adfs, so admin_dc and admin_adfs can never be reached. The only remaining source of stolen_dbadmin is the ciserver leak (auth_ciserver, escalate with stolen_admin, leak_secrets_ciserver). mfa:dbadmin then blocks auth_db, auth_fileserver and auth_backup, because the only bypass needs admin_dc or admin_adfs. Why one control is not enough: with mfa:admin alone, the ciserver leak gives stolen_dbadmin and auth_db succeeds. With mfa:dbadmin alone, the DC path gives admin_dc, which bypasses mfa_dbadmin. Segmented alone fails because escalate_workstation provides the admin bypass for pivot_internal. Patching one workstation or laptop leaves the other phishing path open. The other patches only shut off redundant routes. So the minimum is 2.\"}"
    }
   ],
